  "scope": "For each fixed logarithmic spacing h>0, one proved but unevaluated E_h bounds the squarefree mean of a whole signed cutoff family by E_h*X*sum a_i^2, independently of family size and cutoff magnitudes. The exact complementary endpoint is floor((n-1)/R). Rounding loses at most one in logarithmic separation; direct and complementary divisor pairs each have one finite interval error, and these errors are absorbed into the same summable separation kernel. No separate counting-error term remains. S is any squarefree nonunit selection in (1,X]; the budget uses X, not the cardinality or short-interval length. The original varying owner allocation and cofactor-dependent interval endpoints cost only 36*(b+1)^2 for 2^b periods, with the same linear population and coefficient-energy budget. Both signed bounds and the literal cutoffSlope bridge retain strict rounding, original logarithmic owner shares and radial lengths T(n,i). A nonnegative decreasing cofactor-dependent factor bounded by D has only the squared D price, with no additional period or maximal-depth loss. The exact reciprocal factor 1/(T(n,i)-log n) retains a separate positive lower bound delta(n) inside the energy sum (w(n)/delta(n))^2. This does not replace actual prime moments by their density or bound their irregular signed error. Base coefficients a_i and cutoffs R_i are fixed across labels; common real weights remain arbitrary with explicit unpaid weight energy. Arbitrary a_i(n) and cofactor-dependent noncontiguous period holes are not covered. The remaining actual prime-moment coefficient dependence, joint signed cutoff correction and total source-normalized cost are open. No zero or simplicity hypothesis; preserve disjoint paid sectors. Both whole -79/1000 floor and 3/2 ceiling remain open; no zero exclusion or RH claim.",
